# `yo fix <path> --migrate explicit-copy` judges a copy made at a result
# position of an `if`/`cond`/`match` (an arm's tail, a block's tail, nested)
# against the variable the result is bound to
# (issues/fixed/2026-10-22-explicit-copy-migration-clones-a-conditional-arm-whose-binding-is-written-afterwards.md).
# `target := if(negated, entry.no, entry.yes); target.push(v)` aliases one of
# the entry's lists on purpose, as `add_where_clause_constraint_to_env` does
# in src/env.yo; the migration used to clone both arms (7 insertions), and
# the run printed `0 0 0`. Now the five `target` arm copies are listed as
# "the copy is written afterwards", and only `seen`'s two (never written)
# are cloned. The trailing `build run` runs the migrated program: `2 2 2` is
# the behavior the rewrite must keep. stdout_keep drops the build chatter.
stdout_keep=^(fix:|[0-9]+ [0-9]+ [0-9]+$)
timeout=600
